{% extends 'demos/live_demo.html.twig' %}

{% block demo_container %}

    <div class="max-w-[1140px] mx-auto mb-12">
        <twig:Browser class="shadow-blur shadow-blur--rainbow w-full">
            <twig:PaginationPlayground />
        </twig:Browser>
    </div>

    <section class="py-12" style="padding-top: 6rem;">
        <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
            <twig:Code:CodeWithExplanationRow filename="src/Twig/Components/PaginationPlayground.php" reversed sticky>
Every control of the playground is a writable `LiveProp`. Nothing here is
specific to theming: the theme is one argument of `ux_pagination()`, resolved
against a whitelist of templates, and the navigation window is one call on
the builder in `createPagination()`.

The accent color only touches CSS custom properties: the default theme is
styled entirely through `--ux-pagination-*` variables.
            </twig:Code:CodeWithExplanationRow>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-12">
        <div class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto">
            <twig:Code:CodeWithExplanationRow filename="templates/components/PaginationPlayground.html.twig" sticky>
Each form control binds its `LiveProp` with `data-model`, and the component
re-renders on change: new theme, new colors, new navigation window.

The pagination links themselves go through `link_attributes`, so paging
stays instant too. And the template of the selected theme is displayed
below the result, unmodified: what you see is what you would copy into
your app.
            </twig:Code:CodeWithExplanationRow>
        </div>
    </section>

{% endblock %}
